\description{
\code{trop()} calculates the average treatment effect on the treated (ATT) with
the TROP estimator of Athey, Imbens, Qu, and Viviano (2026). A cell is one
unit in one period. For each treated cell, a model calculates the outcome
without the treatment. The model has two-way fixed effects and a low-rank
term with a nuclear-norm penalty, and it uses only untreated cells. Unit
weights decrease with the distance between the outcome history of a unit and
the treated unit. Time weights decrease with the distance in periods.
}
\section{Methods}{

\itemize{
\item The local method (\code{method = "local"}) is the estimator of Eq. 12 and
Algorithm 2 of the paper. Each treated cell has its own unit weights, time
weights, and fit. The leave-one-out criterion of Eq. 4 and Eq. 5 selects
the tuning parameters. The coordinate search follows footnote 2 of the
paper. You can use this method for block, staggered, and general designs.
\item The global method (\code{method = "global"}) is the estimator of the Python
package from the authors. It calculates one fit for a block design. The
time weights decrease with the distance from the center of the treated
block. The unit weights decrease with the distance from the average
treated unit before the treatment. Placebo cross-validation on untreated
units selects the tuning parameters. The coordinate search starts at the
means of the grids.
}
}

\section{Designs}{

In a block design, all treated units start the treatment in the same period
and stay treated to the end of the panel. In a staggered design, the start
periods are different, but the treatment does not stop. In a general design,
the treatment stops for one or more units.
}

\section{TWFE imputation}{

With \code{lambda = c(time = 0, unit = 0, nn = Inf)}, both methods give TWFE
imputation. This method fits unit effects and period effects to the
untreated cells only. Then it subtracts the fitted value from the outcome of
each treated cell.

A conventional TWFE regression is different. It uses all cells, and it has
one coefficient for the treatment. In a block design with no \code{NA} outcomes,
the two results are the same. In a staggered design, the results can be
different if the effects are different between treated cells. The regression
can give negative weights to some treated cells.
}

\section{Inference}{

The unit bootstrap of Algorithm 3 of the paper gives the standard error. The
bootstrap keeps the tuning parameters fixed. With \code{resample = "stratified"},
the bootstrap draws treated and untreated units separately. With
\code{resample = "controls"}, the treated units do not change. The standard error
is the standard deviation of the draws.

With \code{strata}, the bootstrap draws the units of each stratum separately.
Each draw keeps the number of treated units and of untreated units in each
stratum. Use this when the units are in groups, for example a rollout in
waves.

With \code{boot_rows}, you give the draws. Use \code{\link[=trop_boot_rows]{trop_boot_rows()}} to make them.
If you give the same draws to two or more fits, the fits have a joint
bootstrap distribution. Then \code{\link[=trop_stepdown]{trop_stepdown()}} can adjust the p-values of
the fits. The draws and the panel are in the result, so \code{\link[=trop_effects]{trop_effects()}} can
also calculate the effects of groups of cells.

The default interval is the normal interval with this standard error, as in
the bootstrap variance of the paper. With \code{ci = "percentile"}, the interval
comes from quantiles of the draws, as in the Python package from the
authors. A percentile interval is less accurate with a small number of
draws. The paper also says that the bootstrap is correct only with many
treated units. Thus, the function gives a warning message for a bootstrap
with fewer than 5 treated units.
}

\section{Target cells}{

By default, the estimate is the mean of the effects of all treated cells.
With \code{target}, the estimate uses only some of the treated cells. For
example, use \code{target} for the first 3 periods after the start of the
treatment. The function does not use the outcome of a treated cell outside
of the target, because the treatment can change it. The outcome is not in the
fit of any other cell, and the cell is not in the estimate.

The result has the same value as a fit with \code{NA} in the outcome of these
cells. The element \code{n_excluded_cells} of the result has the number of these
cells.

The target is only for the local method, because the global method fits all
treated cells together. Use \code{\link[=trop_effects]{trop_effects()}} to calculate the effects of
many groups of cells in one fit.
}

\section{Tuning grid}{

The default grid of \code{\link[=trop_grid]{trop_grid()}} fits an outcome with a standard deviation
of about 1. If the outcome has other units, a better value can be outside
of the grid. Then cross-validation chooses a value at the edge of the grid.
The function gives a warning message when this happens for a finite value of
\code{time}, \code{unit}, or \code{nn}. The result has the edge values in \code{cv$boundary}.

With \code{grid = "auto"}, the function scales the default grid to the data with
\code{\link[=trop_grid_scaled]{trop_grid_scaled()}}. With \code{demean = TRUE}, the function subtracts the mean
of the untreated outcomes of each unit before it calculates the distances
between units. The unit effect absorbs a constant of a unit. Thus, with
\code{unit = 0} the effects do not change. With a larger \code{unit} weight, the
weights of the units are different. They compare the shape of the outcome
histories and not the level.
}

\section{Event times}{

\code{\link[=trop_effects]{trop_effects()}} and \code{\link[=trop_cells]{trop_cells()}} can make groups by event time and by
cohort. By default, they find the start of each treatment run from \code{W}.
The event time 0 is the first treated period of the run.

With \code{event_time}, you give the event time of each treated cell. Then the
groups use these values, and the cohort of a cell is the period with the
event time 0. Use the column \code{event_time} of \code{\link[=trop_rollout]{trop_rollout()}}.

The event time counts the periods of the panel. In a run of treated periods,
it must increase by 1 from one period to the next. Thus, all cells of a run
have the same cohort. A new run after an untreated period can have a
different start. The function stops with an error if the event times do not
agree with the runs of \code{W}.

The periods of the panel are the values of \code{time} in \code{data}. If no unit has
a row for a period, the panel does not have this period. Then make the
event times from the same data, or add a row with an \code{NA} outcome for this
period.

Use \code{event_time} when the panel has no row for the start of a run. Also use
it when the anticipation periods start before the first period of the
panel. With \code{event_time}, do not give \code{anticipation} to \code{\link[=trop_effects]{trop_effects()}} and
\code{\link[=trop_cells]{trop_cells()}}. The event times already count the anticipation periods.

A cell without a row has no outcome. In \code{trop()}, such a cell gets the
treatment of its unit in the rows before and after it. Thus, a period
without a row does not stop a treatment run. With \code{event_time}, the
treated rows after the cell can show that their run starts after the cell.
Then the cell gets \code{W = 0}, and a new run starts after it.

If the data have no rows just before the first treated row of a run, the
start of the run is not certain. Then the function gives a warning message,
unless you give \code{event_time}.
}

\section{Cross-validation scores}{

The element \code{cv$trace} has one row for each tuning value that the search
examined. The column \code{score} is the criterion. The column \code{n_valid} counts
the cells in the score. For the local method, these are the held-out
untreated cells with a prediction. For the global method, these are the
placebo cells with an effect. The element \code{cv$n_cells} is the number of
all such cells.

A score that uses fewer cells is not comparable. Thus, the search first
prefers the tuning values with the largest \code{n_valid}. With the same
\code{n_valid}, the smaller score is better. The function gives a warning if
the chosen values do not use all cells. If no tuning value has a usable
score, the function stops with an error.
}

\section{Limits of the triple robustness result}{

Theorem 5.1 of the paper gives a bound for the bias. The bound is the product
of three terms: the unit imbalance, the time imbalance, and the bias of the
regression adjustment. The proof uses fixed weights, and it is only for a
block design. For a staggered or general design, the function calculates
the estimator, but the bound has no proof. Thus, the function gives a
warning message for these designs.
}

\section{Covariates}{

Section 6.2 of the paper adds a covariate term to the model of Eq. 13. The
model has the fixed effects, the low-rank term, and a linear term
\eqn{X_{it}'\phi}. Use \code{covariates} to give the names of numeric columns. A
row with an outcome must have finite values for all covariates. For a
factor, make indicator columns with \code{\link[stats:model.matrix]{stats::model.matrix()}}.

The paper gives the model, but it does not give one procedure to calculate
\eqn{\phi}. The function has two procedures:
\itemize{
\item With \code{covariate_method = "joint"}, each weighted fit calculates
\eqn{\phi} together with the fixed effects and the low-rank term. This is
the minimization of Eq. 13. The local method calculates one \eqn{\phi} for
each treated cell. The unit weights and the time weights use the outcomes,
not the outcomes minus \eqn{X'\phi}. The leave-one-out fits and the
placebo fits also calculate \eqn{\phi}.
\item With \code{covariate_method = "residualize"}, the function first calculates
\eqn{\phi} with an unweighted TWFE regression on all untreated cells with
an outcome. Then it uses \eqn{Y^* = Y - \tilde{X}\phi} for the tuning,
the weights, and the estimate. In \eqn{\tilde{X}}, the function removes
the unit effects and the period effects of the untreated cells from
\eqn{X}. The bootstrap calculates \eqn{\phi} again for each draw.
}

Section 6.2 of the paper tunes the estimator after it removes the
covariates from the outcomes. The procedure \code{"residualize"} is one
interpretation of that text. Usually, it does not give the minimum of
Eq. 13. Its first step uses all untreated outcomes. Thus, the cells that the
cross-validation holds out have an effect on \eqn{\phi}.

The global method with covariates is an extension of fasttrop. The Python
package from the authors does not have covariates.

The fixed effects absorb a term \eqn{a_i + b_t} in a covariate. Thus, the
results do not change when you add a unit constant or a period constant to a
covariate.

The function does not use a covariate that the unit effects, the
period effects, or other covariates explain completely on the untreated
cells. For example, a covariate that does not change in time has no effect
on the estimate. The function gives a warning message for such a covariate.
In a weighted fit with few untreated cells, a covariate can also have no
variation. Then, that fit sets its coefficient to 0.

The element \code{covariates$coef} of the result contains \eqn{\phi} in the
units of the covariates. For the local method with \code{"joint"}, it is the mean
of the values for the treated cells. This value is only a summary.

The appendix of the paper gives a bias bound with covariates. This bound
adds a term: the imbalance of the covariates times the error in \eqn{\phi}.
Thus, the weights can decrease the bias, but they do not remove the bias
from a wrong \eqn{\phi}. A covariate that the treatment changes can also
make a bias. Use only covariates that the treatment does not change.
}

